{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added a per-user feed limit (`max_feeds`, 0 = unlimited). The build and existing tests pass (53 unit packages, 102 integration tests); the new behaviour itself has not been exercised by any test I could run.\n\nWhat changed:\n- **Schema and model**: new migration adds `users.max_feeds int not null default 0`; `User` and `UserModificationRequest` carry `max_feeds`, and every full user query and `UpdateUser` reads/writes it.\n- **Who can change it**: in `PUT /v1/users/{userID}`, a non-admin sending a `max_feeds` different from the current value gets 403. Negative values are rejected with 400.\n- **Enforcement**: `storage.CreateFeed` is the single place feeds are inserted, so the limit is enforced there. It locks the user row, counts the user's feeds and inserts in one transaction, returning `storage.ErrFeedLimitReached` when the user is at the limit. This covers the API, OPML import, the web UI and the Google Reader API, and concurrent requests cannot exceed the limit.\n- **`POST /v1/feeds`**: returns 400 when the limit is reached, checked before any network fetch so an unreachable feed URL still gives 400 rather than 500.\n- **`POST /v1/import`**: imports feeds until the limit is hit, then stops and returns 400. Feeds imported before that point are kept, so the import is partial rather than all-or-nothing.\n\nThings you might trip over:\n- The 403 for a non-admin changing `max_feeds` is my choice; the existing `is_admin` check in the same handler returns 400 instead.\n- The two new error messages (limit reached, negative value) are plain English strings, not keys in the translation files.\n- `max_feeds` is not exposed in the web UI or the Go `client` package.\n- I updated the `@effects` tags on `storage.CreateFeed`, `opml.Import` and `importFeedsHandler` to add `reads users`, but not on the two UI OPML handlers that call `Import`.", "t": 1794462866.646}
